@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Stochastic Gradient Descent — From Robbins-Monro to Adam
' Paper: A Stochastic Approximation Method (Robbins & Monro, 1951 — the convergence licence)
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177729586
' Paper: Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization (Kingma & Ba, ICLR 2015 — the adaptive step)
' URL:   https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980
' Level: 1

component "Minibatch\nnoisy estimate of the gradient" as BATCH <<async>>
component "Stochastic approximation\nstep size schedule a_t" as SA <<app>>
component "Momentum\naccumulate a velocity term" as MOM <<app>>
component "Adam\nbias-corrected 1st + 2nd moments,\nper-parameter step" as ADAM <<host>>
database  "Parameters θ" as THETA <<db>>

BATCH ARROW_MAIN SA : one step per batch
SA ARROW_MAIN THETA : θ ← θ - a_t · ∇Loss
SA ARROW_OPTIONAL MOM : damp oscillation\nacross ravines
MOM ARROW_OPTIONAL ADAM : plus per-parameter scaling
ADAM ARROW_MAIN THETA
THETA ARROW_OPTIONAL BATCH : next minibatch

note bottom of SA
  **Robbins-Monro (1951) is the licence:** a noisy
  gradient still converges, provided the step sizes
  shrink neither too fast nor too slowly — Σa_t
  diverges while Σa_t² converges. Every minibatch
  method inherits that guarantee; the batch is a
  cheap unbiased estimate, not an approximation to
  apologize for.
  ----
  **Adam (2015)** keeps running estimates of the
  first and second moments of the gradient and scales
  each parameter's step by them, which is why it
  tolerates badly-scaled features and sparse
  gradients with little tuning. Its distinctive
  component is **bias correction**: both moment
  estimates start at zero and are therefore biased
  toward zero early on, so each is divided by
  1 − β^t before use. The two are biased by different
  amounts (β₂ is nearer 1), and the second moment sits
  under a square root, so //uncorrected// the first step
  is far too **large** — about (1−β₁)/√(1−β₂) ≈ 3.2×
  the intended size at the defaults, worse as β₂ → 1.
  ----
  Consumes the gradients produced by
  ""../neural-networks/backpropagation.puml"".
end note
@enduml
